 "title": "Refusing Free Labour in Interviews",
 "topic": "mindset",
 "summary": "This video is a roleplay demonstrating how to handle companies that ask job candidates to do extensive unpaid work or business cases as part of the interview process. The creator shows how to decline free labor professionally by setting boundaries around your time and expertise.",
 "key_points": [
  "Recognize when a take-home interview assignment is overly extensive and resembles real-world company work.",
  "State clearly that you are not interested in free labor and know the value of your time and experience.",
  "Offer to send your hourly rate for completing the work if the company wishes to leverage your skill set.",
  "Maintain professionalism while setting boundaries to protect your time during the job search."
 ],
 "content": "Post text: Toodaloo! Transcript: When the company tries to get away with this... [Person 1]: Congratulations, you've made it to the next round of interviews. [Person 2]: That is great news. Thank you. [Person 1]: I'm sending you a use case now and the next portion of the interview process is to come up with a business case for a solution to this company problem. [Person 2]: Confirming I have received it. Oh, wow, this is quite lengthy. [Person 1]: Yes, it's a real life business scenario. So we ask you to keep this confidential as you work on it. [Person 2]: This looks quite involved. [Person 1]: Yes, it is quite extensive, which is why you have the week to complete it. [Person 2]: This looks like something you would hire someone to work on... How would my business case be used? [Person 1]: That is up to the discretion of the company as the company will own all interview materials. [Person 2]: If I'm going to be applying my skills and working out a proposition to solve a real company issue, I will be owning all the materials and they will be watermarked and a consulting fee would apply should the company wish to implement my suggestions. Respectfully, I know the value of my time and experience and while I appreciate making it to the next round of interviews, I'm not interested in free labour. [Person 1]: Well, it could lead you to getting the position, so it's not exactly free labour. [Person 2]: I'm happy to send you my hourly rate for completing this work if the company is interested in leveraging my skill set for this. [Person 1]: [unclear]. [Person 2]: I'll send over all the details in an email and I look forward to working together. [Person 1]: [unclear]. [Person 2]: Toodaloo. Text on screen: When the company tries to get away with this...",
